#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "b8306278-726a-5c09-86cc-14631a6abb66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12659" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 - 5x_2^2 +3x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that \n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 3x_1^2, \\qquad -5x_2^2 \\leq 3x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 3.$\n\nNotice that when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/1986d7b40e318db91ac16ba9540ec704fd6221ac295dec66540c190a090a1463.png) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2}+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "5b4117f3-62fb-55c6-8e23-0cadcd4a2ec5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12660" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=5x_1^2 - 4x_2^2 - 6x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5x_1^2 \\geq -6x_1^2, \\qquad -4x_2^2 \\geq -6x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-6(1)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq -6.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no smaller than $-6.$ But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = -6.$\n\nNotice that when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/d509eb79bc8d9a96b60922166e49d0225eac7a99d2614859c31af15199d4d47c.png) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}-4(0)^{2}-6(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "3cec995b-8918-5c48-927e-b0807a947518" :question/math-academy-id "e-12661" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2-2x_3^2+2x_2x_3$  given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/d7716e2c2924212e880275dd674d4a951daf66ac41e5a888a57acb6be686118f.png)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83813b32-f311-5992-b44c-076128c2c81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367080} {:answer/id #uuid "8441445f-64c5-5f3b-aac5-f12f05b4e46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367081} {:answer/id #uuid "11ea8215-c2b6-5b52-b3ef-7501347dd312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367082} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5545d9-f3a7-52ee-aaa7-82fce7932a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367083} {:answer/id #uuid "50307521-32b9-5e89-ab20-d9b7f80322e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb5545d9-f3a7-52ee-aaa7-82fce7932a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367083} :answer-field/id #uuid "810d4f20-4fff-54ff-a388-dc08b8219321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d163581a-379b-56c8-98a1-48aed6d35231" :question/math-academy-id "q-138018" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(1)^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8c9efcd-1247-5933-b64b-346934135478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163653} {:answer/id #uuid "bca7c8f1-df96-567a-bcff-8bc7fe59518e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163654} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7ac1a3-c2a9-596d-9cdb-1dbfcef22bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163655} {:answer/id #uuid "d49eed47-fdf5-559f-a327-b2be458ae699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163656} {:answer/id #uuid "d7063632-c6d7-5eb1-9581-a84f5f0b9777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c9efcd-1247-5933-b64b-346934135478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163653} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c8ea6e-b51f-5f03-855e-98e7ff8988c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "657b8539-11db-5516-89dc-20b2cb407f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-138019" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17164810-98d4-5619-9cb1-63918896c640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367087} {:answer/id #uuid "910c15ce-4050-59b0-8bfe-c153c3e9f55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367088} {:answer/id #uuid "6aeb03d3-128e-5b82-b5a2-274e33c406c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367089} {:answer/id #uuid "7171401c-7e56-522d-9f55-d66d06fb6ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367090} {:answer/id #uuid "c80e609b-891c-539c-a126-4812f5c666ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910c15ce-4050-59b0-8bfe-c153c3e9f55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367088} :answer-field/id #uuid "8847a1c5-2d26-535d-bd3d-b2707a0c54d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78039968-7f65-5b7c-bc19-419760690ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138023" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d145fba2-acb3-56f2-9f48-0f09f260ee4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367094} {:answer/id #uuid "e5499d20-c5b5-5532-a813-9537f78c6116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367095} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b8b977-6e39-53be-b4da-d34761c5d4cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367096} {:answer/id #uuid "64e7e46d-326a-5593-b01c-ca195e51a5ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367097} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec6758d-d203-5c7d-afa2-7e15c53f12d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5499d20-c5b5-5532-a813-9537f78c6116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367095} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bd729-b8dc-556b-b331-d0a1a47a64ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c0412d6-580b-5534-97ba-10917d9a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138029" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 2{x}_{1}^{2},\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}+3(0)^{2}+2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5593af0-9696-5471-9677-b1a9d7c1c163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367101} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca4b54b-6647-5028-855c-870a5359746e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367102} {:answer/id #uuid "78045ed6-ca34-5f81-8667-1a655fbaf95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367103} {:answer/id #uuid "5723b1b4-0bee-5d2e-b437-1814dc68d1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367104} {:answer/id #uuid "7d273c53-c4bc-5668-8108-bf73ee8be2dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d273c53-c4bc-5668-8108-bf73ee8be2dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367105} :answer-field/id #uuid "95310221-de35-5754-aa26-fd9ea199694f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa4bb6b4-8b09-52db-958b-3d34503b56c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138064" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -2.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-2.$ But before we conclude that $-2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b680c18-9c6b-550c-9b08-a609bbe208b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367108} {:answer/id #uuid "54b0e57c-b70b-5fbe-ac9f-9b32c7bde4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367109} {:answer/id #uuid "14522301-01af-583b-b89d-25b17c705bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367110} {:answer/id #uuid "0add60c4-4aa7-59f5-8faf-b0058826fb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367111} {:answer/id #uuid "2731b785-d188-52a4-a776-167905787c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b0e57c-b70b-5fbe-ac9f-9b32c7bde4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367109} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8981759-b67d-5478-972f-51259def7b1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51fe0971-9f79-57cb-b9e7-66881287b5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138065" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,5{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$   \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3(0)^{2}-4(1)^{2}+5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c1eff4-4d09-5e63-9cc7-93ac36c0fda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367115} {:answer/id #uuid "c9866f0e-a387-5bad-9ada-e4632702336c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367116} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89b0fc-5ab5-507a-95be-a4bf9f9f1390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367117} {:answer/id #uuid "72a125f3-b1d4-50cb-9c69-63e62dd88adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367118} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ca2593-0378-5c60-a8ac-15b9840baca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ca2593-0378-5c60-a8ac-15b9840baca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367119} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed53c7b7-1529-5f0d-8cda-6c16d362dc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3655d4ba-053a-5c00-a9f4-82a3ece073b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138066" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e6f462-0a65-51ff-94e3-e7ba60e72746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367122} {:answer/id #uuid "62c0a855-d71d-50cf-a956-2f94e9bd4886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367123} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6492cd-b48c-55e9-a487-02e991dce708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367124} {:answer/id #uuid "778e9be3-d2fe-58ad-b5f6-1e9c821fdf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367125} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b854e0-0950-51a5-8e8c-8da0c1c7b103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e6f462-0a65-51ff-94e3-e7ba60e72746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367122} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb76961c-25fe-5784-817c-45d36c71bb72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e071b0c8-e066-5846-8bcb-b339254a2972" :question/math-academy-id "q-138067" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -21 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=-7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed74a3dd-5690-5488-b52d-4d9e6e9c1eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367129} {:answer/id #uuid "c0bce4b2-1c5c-52e3-b58b-756020408fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367130} {:answer/id #uuid "066cd44d-0ef6-5af3-ba5e-1462b4b7f939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367131} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4ce460-4506-5946-a8ab-04c4709d615d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367132} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d4865a-8a57-57e9-a7ff-7cbbe7eed620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066cd44d-0ef6-5af3-ba5e-1462b4b7f939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367131} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb62a26-e526-5907-81fb-7294f7d3759f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "469bd1b0-4e94-52f9-a547-6512114b725e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138068" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +21 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -7) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfbec8fd-5d7c-5fee-8786-ff730668bae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367136} {:answer/id #uuid "888a766c-2798-534a-a658-964299963501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367137} {:answer/id #uuid "f46f76ae-6bdd-57d1-b1f6-2cde819e1711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367138} {:answer/id #uuid "0a67c3d0-0bc5-5fdb-a7a7-2718058ca368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367139} {:answer/id #uuid "73308c35-5633-5880-9a28-c40549a14bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888a766c-2798-534a-a658-964299963501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367137} :answer-field/id #uuid "e109208c-16e5-518b-bc9b-8f254dfedede" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4846f1d2-4ccf-5b3b-802d-646a9deabd39" :question/math-academy-id "q-138070" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118e4b71-542f-594f-9291-e6ce6c19212a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367143} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e9d7ba-b763-528d-8faf-415aa2a81d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367144} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d01c79-9e93-5ebc-9a15-16d49ff3c2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367145} {:answer/id #uuid "8e19bbca-8283-5e80-9cd8-32b44eb81680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367146} {:answer/id #uuid "5453e701-bc1b-54d1-8e0d-14d98c394a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5453e701-bc1b-54d1-8e0d-14d98c394a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367147} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b97668-97d4-51da-9f38-49ef169cc54d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cf5ef63-0b32-59cb-896f-6647575d6bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138074" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a270ff22-7d68-5ff8-86c3-a7a665ad39f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367150} {:answer/id #uuid "08e8ae0f-8a3f-5049-9f38-4faa202a719d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367151} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb3965e-6e1a-59b7-bdb5-c272462a48b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367152} {:answer/id #uuid "98e6052c-b92f-532b-9242-b5367a877201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367153} {:answer/id #uuid "31f3fa42-a72f-54f3-b5ce-d3f63aabddf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb3965e-6e1a-59b7-bdb5-c272462a48b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367152} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a3bf9b0-e466-575e-89f1-8fa1dcdf6adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5fd20d1-98db-517c-aace-d5d1d5f811ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-158468" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=8(1)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 8.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $8.$ But before we conclude that $8$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=8.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}+8(1)^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c87827-91a8-546d-8923-32a540591d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367157} {:answer/id #uuid "4172d112-013f-5d5e-a98b-114a078595ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367158} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac933eb-d9df-55c0-a75e-b163a81f890c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367159} {:answer/id #uuid "92316891-7206-5cd5-8985-03193a21ca48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367160} {:answer/id #uuid "8e117736-f7b3-5081-9b4c-7d0507b407a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ac933eb-d9df-55c0-a75e-b163a81f890c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367159} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c73e17-a0bf-570c-82bd-8252f9c5b89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa85658-d069-5afe-b717-6061c2186693" :question/math-academy-id "q-158526" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "717ababa-dfa9-5609-b82a-07dea2dec911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367164} {:answer/id #uuid "68f0d4a5-20a9-5e68-b2fc-6e79f1849d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367165} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf8022b-2437-5927-9c56-bda2b1b38f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367166} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd12d9b-14dc-53a2-bfd9-8e515a357d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367167} {:answer/id #uuid "e4739964-bb68-5cce-b6d0-e5eb2cc92c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd12d9b-14dc-53a2-bfd9-8e515a357d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9d7608-1263-586d-a287-b81d95e22506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57626bf6-6fb8-577b-860a-222c6da3f8a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158528" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-7{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-7(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ecac5c1-0865-5d5d-9e5e-34a181f0835c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367171} {:answer/id #uuid "090a3b2c-56ef-5d6e-abdf-4480eb5b50ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367172} {:answer/id #uuid "73a23f19-3f7c-584d-ba30-6f780b41678a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367173} {:answer/id #uuid "5916a263-8ee6-5c22-9977-82d2d2b23f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367174} {:answer/id #uuid "2a29ebf1-af39-5367-a49d-bd82553153d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "090a3b2c-56ef-5d6e-abdf-4480eb5b50ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367172} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a383cf3-bb93-59ee-a8e3-508831b9676f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc65d907-e952-54f8-94c3-91f4719ec6d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158529" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$"}]}
